// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-09-08 (Tuesday) — Carnatic melakarta math
// Melakarta #57: Simhendramadhyamam
// ============================================================================
//
// THE NUMBERING SYSTEM (chakra/position scheme, same real combinatorial
// structure used on 2026-08-25's #22 and 2026-09-01's #20 — not katapayadi
// this time, but the brief's own "another real melakarta-numbering scheme"):
//   chakra   = ceil(N / 6),  position = N - 6*(chakra - 1)
// For N = 57: chakra = ceil(57/6) = 10 -> Ri-Ga combo (chakras 7-12 repeat
//             chakras 1-6's combos, base index (10-6)=4) = R2 G2 (chatushruti
//             Ri, sadharana Ga)
//             position = 57 - 6*9 = 3 -> Dha-Ni combo #3 = D1 N3 (shuddha
//             Dha, kakali Ni)
//             N > 36 -> prati Ma (M2), not shuddha
// melakarta 57 = S R2 G2 M2 P D1 N3. Semitones (S=0 R1=1 R2/G1=2 R3/G2=3 G3=4
// M1=5 M2=6 P=7 D1=8 D2/N1=9 D3/N2=10 N3=11): 0 2 3 6 7 8 11.
// -> verified against tonaljs's own scale-type data (same discipline as the
//    Bhairav/"gypsy" and prior Tuesdays' matches): interval formula
//    "1P 2M 3m 4A 5P 6m 7M" = 0 2 3 6 7 8 11 is EXACTLY tonaljs's "hungarian
//    minor" scale. This identity is also independently documented for
//    Simhendramadhyamam itself (ragajunglism.org: "equivalent to the
//    Hungarian minor scale, also called the Gypsy minor scale in Western
//    music") — an honest match, not a "close enough" guess.
//    -> .scale("A:hungarian:minor") (tonic A, a fresh pitch center — G was
//       used twice this week already, 09-01 and 09-07).
// The scale's defining feature: TWO augmented-second gaps (3 semitones each,
// not the usual 1 or 2) — G2->M2 (degrees 2->3) and D1->N3 (degrees 5->6).
// This is the actual "vivadi" (dissonant-interval) character Carnatic
// theory names Simhendramadhyamam for — an exotic, Middle-Eastern-flavored
// sound, not a shade of major/minor like #20 (aeolian) or #22 (dorian) were.
// Scale degree map in "A:hungarian:minor":
//   0=S(A) 1=R2(B) 2=G2(C) 3=M2(D#) 4=P(E) 5=D1(F) 6=N3(G#) 7=S'(A5)
//
// A THIRD NUMBER, from N itself: digit sum 5+7 = 12 -> drives the delay time
// below, so N's math shows up three separate ways (chakra, position, digit
// sum), not just cycle count.
//
// ARRANGEMENT — deliberately NOT a repeat of 09-01's recipe (kick-locked
// wobble sub-bass, no separate drone, sawtooth lead, techno/dubstep drum
// alternation). What's actually distinctive about THIS melakarta is the
// exotic double-augmented-second interval itself, and the classical way to
// make an unusual raga's color audible is a steady tonic/fifth drone
// underneath it — the Carnatic tambura, same principle as Hindustani tanpura
// (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §3) — so the ear has a fixed reference to hear the
// augmented seconds "sour" against. That's a genuine reason to bring the
// drone convention BACK after 09-01 dropped it for a genre-fusion ask; it's
// not reuse-by-default, it's what this specific raga needs to read as
// exotic rather than just "another minor-ish scale."
//   drone:  sine, tonic + fifth (S/P as a2/e3), tanpura convention, purest
//           waveform per MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5.
//   lead:   square (not sawtooth again — 09-01 and 09-07 both just used
//           sawtooth) for a harder, more nasal edge that suits the scale's
//           dissonant color; fast attack / short decay reads as a plucked
//           attack without a sample. Filter LFO period is chakra (10) ->
//           .slow(10), one slow sweep across most of the pattern; a touch
//           of distortion adds grit appropriate to the "vivadi" tension.
//   NO DRUM KIT — corrected 9 Sep 2026 (see MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md's autopilot-
//   percussion entry). The first draft bolted a TR909 kick/rim/hat stack on
//   just so the three derived numbers (position, chakra, digit sum) each had
//   something to drive audibly — but that reasoning is backwards: a real
//   Carnatic raga exposition over a tambura drone (an alapana) has NO metric
//   drum kit, and this file's own drone rationale two paragraphs up already
//   says so ("the classical way to make an unusual raga's color audible is a
//   steady tonic/fifth drone... so the ear has a fixed reference"). Adding a
//   909 club pulse under that reasoning was decorative, not structural. The
//   three numbers still drive real audible parameters, just not a drum
//   layer: chakra (10) sets the lead filter's sweep period, digit sum (12)
//   sets the delay time, and position (3) now sets the delay feedback amount
//   (.3) below instead of a kick's euclid(3,8).
//
// MELODY — four phrases (call / climax-response / drop / retrograde
// variation), anchored on S and P (the two swaras every melakarta shares,
// the closest Carnatic equivalent to a Hindustani vadi/samvadi pair here):
//   A (call): establishes tonic, climbs into the FIRST augmented second
//     (G2->M2) then leaps to P:            0 ~ 2 3 ~ 4 ~ ~
//   B (response/climax): climbs through the SECOND augmented second
//     (D1->N3), a half-step leading-tone rise to the octave, falls back:
//                                            4 5 6 ~ 7 ~ 6 4
//   C (the drop): the octave falls a tritone to M2 (the scale's sharp 4th —
//     the "vivadi" clash made explicit as a leap, not just a passing tone),
//     then another tritone down resolves to the tonic — tension deliberately
//     exploited, then released:              7 ~ 3 ~ 0 ~ ~ ~
//   D (retrograde variation of A, per MUSIC-QC-LOOP's motif-and-variation
//     device): same four pitches reversed, closing with a leap from BELOW
//     the tonic (degree -1, the octave-down N3 as a leading tone) instead of
//     landing on it directly — a different cadence than any phrase before:
//                                            4 ~ 3 2 ~ -1 0 ~
//
// CYCLES = 20, setcpm(60) -> 20 * 60/60 = 20s. 20 cycles = five full passes
// of the 4-phrase alternation, a clean symmetric loop.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(60)
let CYCLES = 20 // 20 * 60/60 = 20s; five passes of the 4-phrase melody

stack(
  // DRONE — tanpura convention, S + P (tonic/fifth), sine (purest waveform,
  // not a sample — MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5). Slow attack/release so it swells
  // in rather than snapping on, giving the exotic scale a stable bed to be
  // heard against. Gain raised slightly from the first draft (.22 -> .28) now
  // that there's no drum layer to sit under — the drone carries the low end.
  note("<a2 e3>/4")
    .sound("sine")
    .gain(.28)
    .attack(2).release(3)
    .room(4.5),

  // MELODY — see MELODY note above. Real leaps (thirds, tritones, the raga's
  // own augmented seconds, a leap from below the tonic), rests for breathing
  // room, tension->release (C) and retrograde-variation (D) as the required
  // jazz-improv devices.
  n(`<
    0 ~ 2 3 ~ 4 ~ ~
    4 5 6 ~ 7 ~ 6 4
    7 ~ 3 ~ 0 ~ ~ ~
    4 ~ 3 2 ~ -1 0 ~
  >`)
    .scale("A:hungarian:minor")
    .sound("square")
    .attack(.005).decay(.1).sustain(.15).release(.08)
    .cutoff(1500).resonance(10)
    .lpf(sine.range(700, 3400).slow(10)) // chakra (10) -> sweep period
    .distort(.18)
    .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)) // improv hook: occasional stuttered ornament
    .gain(.42)
    .delay(.22)
    .delaytime(1/12) // digit sum of 57 (5+7=12) -> a twelfth-note delay
    .delayfeedback(.3) // position (3) -> delay feedback amount, no drum kit
)
